We sang to him because we couldn't hold him.
我们对他唱歌，因为我们不能抱他。

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:47.359
He, you know, survived an open heart surgery.
他，你知道，在心脏直视手术中幸存了下来。

00:27:47.359 --> 00:27:49.000
He got out and he got home.
他出院回家了。

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.160
And it was so clear that music was his thing.
很明显，音乐是他的强项。

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:55.920
Maybe in part because we had, that was our first way of communicating with him, but he's just naturally so musical.
也许部分原因是我们那是与他交流的第一种方式，但他天生就很有音乐天赋。

00:27:55.920 --> 00:28:00.000
So we do, we would write these songs for how much mother, Carter and I.
所以我们，卡特和我，会为《老爸老妈浪漫史》写这些歌。

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:13.880
And ever since his birth and the realization in his early months, one year old, I would do a thing where if I was the guy originating the song, Carter and I co-wrote a lot of those songs, sometimes one of us did more than the other, but this was the one that I started off.
自从他出生并意识到他才几个月大、一岁时，我就做了一件事：如果我是这首歌的原创者——卡特和我共同创作了其中很多歌，有时我们其中一人做得更多——但这首歌是我开始的。

00:28:13.880 --> 00:28:19.440
And I sort of wrote a lot of music for in the melody and then we kind of wrote the lyrics together about some of the lyrics.
我写了很多旋律，然后我们一起写了歌词，关于其中的一些歌词。

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:24.640
And it really started with me sitting out of bed with the guitar, with Elliot, who was one.
这真的始于我坐在床边拿着吉他，和一岁的埃利奥特在一起。

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:29.160
And he would reach out and strum at my guitar and like just he was fascinated by it.
他会伸出手拨弄我的吉他，他被它迷住了。

00:28:29.160 --> 00:28:39.079
And I had this really concrete and really lovely memory of knowing I had to write this song and just starting to like write melodies around him.
我有一个非常具体且非常可爱的记忆，我知道我必须写这首歌，并开始围绕他写旋律。

00:28:39.079 --> 00:28:42.640
And the melody that made him smile was this one.
让他微笑的旋律就是这一首。

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:43.480
Wow.
哇。

00:28:43.480 --> 00:28:52.519
And I would just absolutely let him decide which way I was gonna steer a lot of these songs that I, the ones that I originated the music for.
对于我原创音乐的那些歌，我绝对会让他决定我该往哪个方向引导。

00:28:52.519 --> 00:28:59.319
And I just have such a clear memory of like, singing this melody to him and him smiling and me knowing this was it.
我有一个非常清晰的记忆，就是对他唱这个旋律，他笑了，我知道就是这个了。

00:28:59.319 --> 00:29:01.480
This was, we were gonna build it all on this.
这就是我们要建立的一切基础。

00:29:01.480 --> 00:29:08.240
And I would write this song and others just with him nearby to like let me know what was working.
我会写这首歌和其他歌，只是让他待在旁边，让我知道什么有效。

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:09.599
He was your radar for what was it?
他是你判断什么的雷达？

00:29:09.599 --> 00:29:11.200
He was my radar, yeah.
他是我的雷达，是的。

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:12.200
It was really cool.
这真的很酷。

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:14.400
Yeah, and it's really, and I wrote the song.
是的，而且真的，我写了这首歌。

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:15.400
He was a year old.
他当时一岁。

00:29:15.400 --> 00:29:16.960
We had been through so much.
我们经历了很多。

00:29:16.960 --> 00:29:21.240
He had been through, you know, so much medically and life or stuff.
他在医疗和生活方面经历了很多。

00:29:21.240 --> 00:29:41.759
And I remember like really pouring my heart into this absurd breakup song called, Cels is in the sand, like there is, this is not a bit, I am being in no way sarcastic or winky, there is legitimate heartfelt emotion in this song, like I really, I really feel something every time I hear it.
我记得我真的把心倾注在这首荒谬的分手歌里，叫《沙堡》，这不是个段子，我一点也不讽刺，也不开玩笑，这首歌里有真正发自内心的情感，每次听到它，我真的都有所感触。

00:29:41.759 --> 00:29:47.880
We play this song when you do our fundraiser concert for, you know, we do a How Much Mother Fundraiser concert in New York City every year, we always play the song.
我们在纽约市每年举办《老爸老妈浪漫史》慈善音乐会时会演奏这首歌，我们总是演奏它。

00:29:47.880 --> 00:29:54.240
It like, it legitimately moves me remembering how, like, much aching emotion I put into say.
每当想起我投入了多少痛苦的情感，它确实让我感动。

00:29:54.240 --> 00:30:04.720
Well, I think that's why, Craig, it has stood the test of time because Robin Sparkles and her team of, presumably, 20 of the best Canadian writers.
嗯，克雷格，我认为这就是它经受住时间考验的原因，因为罗宾·斯帕克尔斯和她那大概20位加拿大顶尖作家团队。

00:30:06.400 --> 00:30:14.960
When they wrote this song, they were trying to go for something that would make teenage teenagers weep.
当他们写这首歌时，他们试图创作出能让十几岁的青少年落泪的东西。

00:30:14.960 --> 00:30:15.460
Yes.
是的。

00:30:15.460 --> 00:30:16.159
Yeah, exactly.
是的，没错。

00:30:16.159 --> 00:30:24.848
And I, like, I remember, like, being near tears while working on this song, like moved to tears by, like how the heartache, I was putting my own like heartache into the song.
而且我记得在创作这首歌时，我快要流泪了，被我投入到歌中的那种心碎感所感动。

00:30:24.848 --> 00:30:34.986
And I would say, I would just give a shout out to one, there were so many, you know, listening to Debbie Gibson, listening to Tiffany, who was then in the video, Tiffany was a huge influence on the song.
我想说，我要特别提一下，当时有很多歌手，比如听黛比·吉布森（Debbie Gibson），听蒂芙尼（Tiffany），她当时也在视频里，蒂芙尼对这首歌的影响非常大。

00:30:34.986 --> 00:30:41.828
But I would say the one that I wanted to give a shout out, like obviously the bangles, Blended Carlisle, all of that stuff, late 80s era.
但我会说我想特别提一下的，显然还有手镯合唱团（The Bangles）、贝琳达·卡莱尔（Belinda Carlisle）等等，都是80年代末的那些东西。

00:30:41.828 --> 00:30:48.820
And one that nobody talks about anymore that I really listened to, maybe the most while writing the song that I also find incredibly moving.
还有一个现在没人提，但我当时真的听了很多，可能是在写这首歌时听得最多的，我也觉得它非常动人。

00:30:48.519 --> 00:30:52.440
So this day is the song, Toy Soldiers by Martika.
这首歌就是玛蒂卡（Martika）的《Toy Soldiers》。

00:30:53.359 --> 00:30:56.160
That I bet do you guys even know what I'm talking about?
我敢说，你们甚至知道我在说什么吗？

00:30:56.160 --> 00:30:57.559
Do you mind to decide?
你介意决定吗？

00:30:57.559 --> 00:31:02.440
I probably toy soldiers by Martika is fucking awesome.
我可能觉得玛蒂卡的《Toy Soldiers》棒极了。

00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:03.759
And it's like heartbreaking.
而且它让人心碎。

00:31:03.759 --> 00:31:05.039
It's it's legitimately.
它确实是。

00:31:05.039 --> 00:31:07.079
It's a great song and it's really heartbreaking.
这是一首很棒的歌，而且真的很让人心碎。

00:31:07.279 --> 00:31:09.839
It's more I think it's more about like drug.
我觉得它更多是关于毒品。

00:31:09.839 --> 00:31:14.720
There's like there's a hint of like drug use and like legitimate danger and addiction and it's not just a breakup.
里面有一点关于吸毒、真正的危险和成瘾的暗示，不仅仅是分手。

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:15.220
sides.
方面。

00:31:15.220 --> 00:31:18.087
It's James Joyce Ian and it's layers, toy soldiers.
这是詹姆斯·乔伊斯式的，它很有层次，《Toy Soldiers》。

00:31:18.087 --> 00:31:19.380
But that was a big one.
但这首歌很重要。

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:23.653
I would listen that over and over and over while I was working on this song.
我在创作这首歌时，一遍又一遍地听它。

00:31:23.653 --> 00:31:27.082
And then Carter came in and we wrote so many lyrics together.
后来卡特（Carter）来了，我们一起写了很多歌词。

00:31:27.082 --> 00:31:31.804
And, you know, it was just like, it was a really, it was a joy to work on this song.
你知道，这真的，创作这首歌是一种乐趣。

00:31:31.804 --> 00:31:38.663
But I would say that this is one of the most emotionally invested I was in any like song I ever wrote for how much mother.
但我会说，这是我为《老爸老妈浪漫史》写的所有歌中，投入情感最多的一首。

00:31:38.663 --> 00:31:40.181
And I hope it comes across.
我希望这种情感能传达出来。

00:31:40.181 --> 00:31:45.522
I think If you listen to that song, door to door, I think you can hear that in it a little bit.
我想如果你从头到尾听那首歌，我想你能从中听出一点那种感觉。

00:31:45.359 --> 00:31:48.559
And Kobe, when she performed it, she was going there.
还有寇碧（Cobie），当她演唱时，她投入了进去。

00:31:48.559 --> 00:31:51.200
Like she was, it's a, it's a sad breakup song.
就像她，这是一首悲伤的分手歌。

00:31:51.200 --> 00:31:55.359
I'm very proud of how it kind of works on that, weirdly works on that level.
我很自豪它在某种程度上，奇怪地在那个层面上起作用了。

00:31:55.359 --> 00:32:02.230
And this old man, he musted me, fell in love with you.
还有这位老人，他必须我，爱上了你。

00:32:02.230 --> 00:32:05.140
New York City.
纽约市。

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:07.539
And now commercials.
现在是广告时间。

00:32:07.539 --> 00:32:16.000
End of commercials?
广告结束了？

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:19.859
Back to show.
回到节目。

00:32:19.859 --> 00:32:33.900
Craig, I'm curious as a songwriter on a show like this, so both for, let's go them all, and this song, the actual episode can't contain the whole song because you don't have enough time, obviously.
克雷格（Craig），作为这类节目的词曲作者，我很好奇，对于《Let's Go to the Mall》和这首歌，实际的剧集无法包含整首歌，因为显然时间不够。

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:36.859
You're not gonna run a three, three and a half minute song.
你不可能放一首三分钟、三分钟半的歌。

00:32:36.859 --> 00:32:39.579
But you knew that somewhere would live, right?
但你知道它会在某个地方存在，对吧？

00:32:39.579 --> 00:32:42.339
It would live somewhere and people would be able to watch it.
它会存在于某个地方，人们能够看到它。

00:32:42.339 --> 00:32:47.500
But like, that must be a really interesting songwriting exercise to think about the economy.
但那一定是一个非常有趣的词曲创作练习，去思考这种经济性。

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:49.220
Which part am I gonna excerpt?
我该摘录哪一部分？

00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:51.140
What am I going to see in this episode?
在这一集中我要展示什么？

00:32:51.140 --> 00:32:52.059
What's the most important?
什么是最重要的？

00:32:52.059 --> 00:32:54.740
What's the funniest, the most moving?
什么是最好笑的，最动人的？

00:32:54.740 --> 00:32:56.980
Yeah, yeah, 100% with both of those.
是的，是的，百分之百是这样。

00:32:56.980 --> 00:33:00.099
And it was painful because you knew there's going to be great things you can't see.
这很痛苦，因为你知道会有很棒的东西你无法看到。

00:33:00.099 --> 00:33:01.940
So we always posted the full song online.
所以我们总是把完整的歌曲发布在网上。

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:03.579
Obviously you can go on YouTube and look it up.
显然你可以去YouTube上搜索。

00:33:03.579 --> 00:33:09.579
The thing that we were so proud of these songs as full like three or four minute songs that we did that we did release.
我们对这些作为完整的三四分钟歌曲感到非常自豪，我们确实发布了它们。

00:33:09.579 --> 00:33:12.460
And I'm still hearing from him and fans that don't know this exists.
我仍然从粉丝那里听到他们不知道这些存在。

00:33:12.460 --> 00:33:19.299
There's a double album of all the original music from how I met your mother, how I met your music volume one and two.
有一张双专辑，收录了《老爸老妈浪漫史》的所有原创音乐，《How I Met Your Music》第一卷和第二卷。

00:33:19.299 --> 00:33:21.380
And the two was like called Deluxe.
第二卷被称为豪华版。

00:33:21.380 --> 00:33:24.579
And there are two full albums of all of these original songs.
有两张完整的专辑收录了所有这些原创歌曲。

00:33:24.579 --> 00:33:26.460
You can get them on all your streaming services.
你可以在所有的流媒体服务上找到它们。

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:29.539
And yeah, people are still finding that to this day.
是的，直到今天人们还在发现它们。

00:33:29.539 --> 00:33:30.380
It's great.
这太棒了。

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:32.700
But we really wanted to compile it for exactly that reason, Josh.
但我们确实想把它们汇编起来，正是出于这个原因，乔什（Josh）。

00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:35.500
We're like, we can't just, these songs can't just go away.
我们想，我们不能，这些歌不能就这样消失。

00:33:35.500 --> 00:33:40.420
You can't just have seen 45 seconds of one of these songs in one TV episode.
你不能只在一部电视剧里看过这些歌的45秒片段。

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:43.900
We were too proud of them, especially when we shot a whole music video too.
我们为它们感到太自豪了，尤其是当我们还拍了完整的音乐录影带时。

00:33:43.900 --> 00:33:44.400
Yeah.
是的。

00:33:44.400 --> 00:33:44.900
Yeah.
是的。

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:51.404
Shout out to Ryan Bethe who was a first year at NYU when I was a third year.
特别感谢瑞安·贝斯（Ryan Bethe），当我是大三学生时，他是纽约大学的大一新生。

00:33:52.539 --> 00:33:53.039
Awesome.
太棒了。

00:33:53.039 --> 00:33:55.013
I didn't, I forgot that if I knew it.
我忘了，如果我知道的话。

00:33:54.940 --> 00:33:58.315
Yeah, I was a little NYU reunion on set, which was really fun.
是的，片场有一个小型的纽约大学聚会，这真的很有趣。

00:33:58.315 --> 00:34:04.140
But I'm curious, like, I don't think the lily thing in her like plays particularly well these days, do you?
但我很好奇，我不认为莉莉（Lily）的那件事在今天看来表现得特别好，你觉得呢？

00:34:04.700 --> 00:34:06.217
Yeah, I worry about it a little.
是的，我有点担心。

00:34:06.217 --> 00:34:15.559
Because it's definitely built on a real thing, but then seeing it enacted, it's like, I could see this bothering some folks and I'm sorry if that's that's really definitely wasn't meant to be that.
因为它绝对是基于真实的事情，但看到它被演绎出来，就像，我能看出这会困扰一些人，如果真是这样我很抱歉，那绝对不是本意。

00:34:15.559 --> 00:34:18.079
Yeah, so I didn't think it aged pretty well.
是的，所以我认为它并没有很好地经受住时间的考验。

00:34:18.079 --> 00:34:26.199
I thought Ryan and Ali did it as well as they could have done, you know, but I just wanted to flag that.
我认为瑞安和艾莉（Ali）已经尽他们所能做到了最好，你知道，但我只是想指出这一点。

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:31.760
I just wanted to go through a couple of things that I flagged that I thought were really funny and charming in this episode.
我只是想过一遍我标记的几件事，我觉得在这一集中非常有趣和迷人。

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:34.440
So I'm in smelling like Drakkar.
比如我闻起来像Drakkar香水。

00:34:34.440 --> 00:34:35.280
Drakkar?
Drakkar？

00:34:35.280 --> 00:34:36.119
See, that's like Courtney can't.
看，这就是考特尼（Courtney）做不到的。

00:34:36.119 --> 00:34:36.960
That's what I mean.
这就是我的意思。

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:38.719
Like Courtney just went back and just had those details.
就像考特尼回去就有了那些细节。

00:34:38.719 --> 00:34:40.320
I guarantee you that's from her original script.
我保证那是来自她最初的剧本。

00:34:40.320 --> 00:34:46.320
Drakkar, Drakkar is the perfect example of like what she has right on her fingertips to put in an episode like this.
Drakkar，Drakkar是一个完美的例子，说明了她手头有什么样的素材可以放进这样的剧集里。

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:50.719
But also like, I don't know a single dude in that era who wasn't wearing Drakkar.
而且，我不认识那个时代有哪个男人不喷Drakkar。

00:34:50.719 --> 00:34:52.110
Drakkar, it was a thing.
Drakkar，那是个潮流。

00:34:52.110 --> 00:34:54.311
Early 90s, Alec, you raised your hand.
90年代初，亚历克（Alec），你举手了。

00:34:54.311 --> 00:34:55.760
You were four in Drakkar.
你四岁时就用Drakkar了。

00:34:57.119 --> 00:34:59.519
I mean, I wish I'd known you then.
我是说，我真希望那时就认识你。

00:34:59.519 --> 00:35:03.119
Everyone was a fan, hard rock, cafe, t-shirts, also a huge thing.
每个人都是粉丝，硬石餐厅（Hard Rock Cafe）的T恤，也是一个巨大的潮流。

00:35:03.119 --> 00:35:03.988
Huge thing, yep.
巨大的潮流，是的。

00:35:03.988 --> 00:35:07.840
These were all just corny details from her first draft, I guarantee it.
我保证，这些都是她初稿里俗气的细节。

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:09.679
She didn't have to change any of that.
她不需要改变任何东西。

00:35:09.679 --> 00:35:12.159
It was like, perfect, absolutely perfect reference.
这就像，完美，绝对完美的参考。

00:35:12.159 --> 00:35:26.579
I always, this happens a lot in the show where you, it's like the cast becomes the writer's room where they're just like, we're sitting around, we're splitting a picture and we're going to come up with 800 Canadian jokes.
我总是，这种情况在节目中经常发生，就像演员阵容变成了编剧室，他们只是，我们坐在一起，分一扎啤酒，然后我们要想出800个关于加拿大的笑话。

00:35:26.579 --> 00:35:29.619
I think we, maybe we own that form.
我想我们，也许我们拥有那种形式。

00:35:29.619 --> 00:35:34.179
You know that fast forward and pop out to hear one thing and then fast-forwarding and pop it in here one thing.
你知道那种快进然后跳出来听一件事，然后再快进跳进去听一件事。

00:35:34.179 --> 00:35:35.380
We do it a lot alone.
我们经常单独做这个。

00:35:35.380 --> 00:35:38.099
We run it another ground a little bit, we did it a lot.
我们把它用得有点过头了，我们做了很多次。

00:35:38.099 --> 00:35:40.659
But I think that's our, I really am proud of that joke.
但我认为那是我们的，我真的为那个笑话感到自豪。

00:35:40.659 --> 00:35:47.300
And I feel like Chris Harris was always a good pit, either he came up with that form or he just was good at pitching on the form.
我觉得克里斯·哈里斯（Chris Harris）一直是个很好的推销员，要么是他想出了那种形式，要么就是他很擅长推销这种形式。

00:35:47.300 --> 00:35:49.820
Just that idea of you just get these little glimpses.
就是那种你只能瞥见一点点的想法。

00:35:49.820 --> 00:35:51.940
I think, I'm very proud whenever we get to that.
我想，每当我们做到这一点时，我都感到非常自豪。

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:54.420
Cause I'm like, I don't see that joke a lot of other places.
因为我觉得我在其他地方没怎么见过那个笑话。

